Carafe Lab: under NCE an alignment fault goes to the process's exception handler as the console's CPU sends it,
instead of Eden running the instruction itself. The Switch's Cortex-A57 faults on every unaligned load-acquire,
store-release and exclusive access, and FEX, which turns x86 loads and stores into such instructions when TSO is
on, rewrites the faulting instruction in its handler; the PC's CPU faults on a subset of them (an access across a
16-byte boundary). Eden's interpreter read register 31 as the stack pointer rather than zero, so a store of zero
(`stlr wzr`) wrote the host's stack pointer to guest memory, and FEX's own path was never run in the lab.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the files it changes.

